From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 02:20:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: histogram/impulse plots of a jump process's visited
 states

X.apply(states).sim(1) (or path.states directly) gives the sequence of
states visited along one sample path, indexed by jump number rather
than clock time. Until now, .plot() could only draw that as a sample
path against its jump index; there was no way to ask "what did the
embedded jump chain look like" or "what fraction of time was spent in
each state" without pulling the values out by hand.

- InfiniteVector.plot() gains type="hist"/type="impulse": drawn from
  self[tmin:tmax] via the existing make_hist/make_impulse renderers,
  reusing the same tmin/tmax bounds the sample-path plot already uses.
  An unsupported type raises a clear ValueError rather than silently
  falling back to a path plot.
- A new weights= parameter (on InfiniteVector.plot, and on make_hist/
  make_impulse themselves) answers the more useful of two different
  questions: an unweighted plot counts how often each state was jumped
  into (the embedded chain's distribution), while weighting each state
  by its holding time -- e.g. path.interarrival_times -- gives the
  fraction of time actually spent in each state, since holding times
  are generally random and state-dependent. make_impulse's counting is
  now backed by a new weighted_count_var() helper alongside the
  existing count_var().
- DiscreteValued gains num_jumps_by(t), converting an elapsed clock
  time into a jump count (by walking get_arrival_times() forward, the
  same way ContinuousTimeMarkovChainResult evaluates itself at a given
  time), so a states plot can be bounded by "everything that happened
  by time T" instead of a fixed number of jumps:
  path.states.plot(tmax=path.num_jumps_by(T), type="impulse").
- RVResults.plot(type=...) now forwards type="hist"/"impulse" to each
  realization's own .plot() for path-shaped (dim is None) results, so
  X.apply(states).sim(n).plot(type="impulse") works directly. Every
  other type value (including "path", which already relies on falling
  through to this same branch untouched) is left unforwarded exactly
  as before, so ordinary path plots are unaffected.
- Tuple.plot(), DiscreteTimeFunction.plot(), and
  ContinuousTimeFunction.plot() gain an explicit type= parameter purely
  to reject anything other than None with a clear, actionable error --
  only a jump process's states (InfiniteVector) support hist/impulse,
  and without this check a stray type= would silently fall through
  **kwargs all the way to matplotlib and surface as an opaque
  "Line2D.set() got an unexpected keyword argument" error far from the
  actual mistake.

Regression tests added in test_result.py (type= dispatch and its
error cases, weights=, num_jumps_by) and test_plot.py (weighted
make_impulse/make_hist directly).
